8. Used in place of the possessive pronoun to denote a personal belonging: as, to hang ''the'' head and weep.

{{smaller block|Is there none of Pygmalion's images . . . to be had now, for putting ''the'' hand in ''the'' pocket?
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., iii. 2. 49.}}

Voltaire is the prince of buffoons: . . . he shakes ''the'' sides; he points ''the'' finger; he turns up ''the'' nose; he shoots out ''the'' tongue. {{right|''Macaulay'', Addison.}}}}

9. Used to denote a particular day in relation to a given week, or to some other day of the same week. [Obsolete or colloq.]

{{smaller block|I m̄ene, if God please, to be at Salisburie ''the'' wekes-daie at night before Easterdaie.
{{right|''Sir J. Popham'' (1582), quoted in H. Hall's Society in the [Elizabethan Age, App. ii.}}

Mrs. Proudie had died on ''the'' Tuesday, . . . and Mr. Robarts had gone over to Silverbridge on ''the'' Thursday.
{{right|''Trollope'', Last Chronicle of Barset, lxviii.}}}}

