11. Used before the relative ''which'': now an archaism.

{{smaller block|Clerkes of holikirke that kepen Crystes tresore,
''The'' which is mannes soul to saue.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), x. 474.}}}}

{{smaller block|[''The'' is generally pronounced as if a syllable (unaccented) of the following word (a proclitic), and its vowel is accordingly obscured, before a consonant, into the neutral vowel-sound of ''her'' or ''but'', very lightly sounded (quite like the French "mute ''e''"); before a vowel, often in the same manner, but more usually with the short ''i'' sound of ''pin'', only less distinct; when emphatic, as the long ''e'' of ''thee''. In poetry, before a word beginning with a vowel-sound, the vowel of ''the'' generally may slide into that of the next word, and form with it one metrical syllable; metrically the ''e'' is accordingly often cut off in printing. The same so-called elision (synalephe) often took place in Middle English, ''the'' being written with the following noun as one word: as, ''themperour'', the emperor.}}

{{smaller block|''Th''' one sweetly flatters, ''th''' other feareth harm.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 172.}}}}

{{smaller block|In Middle English manuscripts ''the'' was often written, as in Anglo-Saxon þe, with the character þ; in early print this character was represented by a form nearly like ''y'', and later printers actually used ''y'' instead, þe, erroneously printed þ<sup>e</sup> as if contracted, like þ<sup>t</sup> for ''that'', being printed ''ye'' or ''y<sup>e</sup>'', but always pronounced, of course, ''the''. Modern archaists often affect ''ye'' for ''the'', and many pronounce it as it looks, "yē."}}

{{smaller block|And on ''ye'' Tewsday at nyght we passed by the yle of Pathemos.
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 14.}}}}

