'''the<sup>2</sup>''' (ᴛ̵ʜē, ᴛ̵ʜẹ̄, or ᴛ̵ʜē̤), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''the'', ''thi'', < AS. ''thē'', ''thȳ'' = OS. ''thiu'', ''diu'', weakened ''te'', ''de'' as an enclitic in ''des te'', ''des de'' = D. ''des te'' = MLG. ''deste'', ''duste'' = MHG. ''deste'', ''dest'', G. ''desto'' (cf. AS. ''thæs the'') = Dan. ''des'', ''desto'' = Sw. ''dess'', ''desto'' = Icel. ''thvī'', ''thī'' = Goth. ''thē'', instr. of ''thata'' (AS. ''thæt''): see ''that'', ''the''<sup>1</sup>.] Used to modify adjectives and adverbs in the comparative degree: {{smaller|(''a'') Correlatively, having in the first instance a relative force, = by how much, and in the second a demonstrative force, = by so much: as, ''the'' sooner ''the'' better; ''the'' more ''the'' merrier.}}

